1
00:00:01,038 --> 00:00:09,028
Hello, my name is Matthew Abate, and today I'm going to be speaking to you about some of the work that we've been doing in the Georgia Tech FACTS Lab and the Georgia Tech Decision and Controls Lab.

2
00:00:09,518 --> 00:00:16,324
The title of my presentation today is Enforcing Safety at Runtime for Systems with Disturbances, and this work was advised by Dr. Sam Coogan.

3
00:00:17,806 --> 00:00:28,292
This work is motivated by control system safety, and the safety problem for control systems is often posed as an invariance constraint, requiring that the system remain in some safe set of operating conditions for all time.

4
00:00:29,774 --> 00:00:35,812
Certain controllers can be verified safe a priori, that is, verify that they render a safe subset of states for an invariant.

5
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:43,236
However, when this is not the case, it can be desirable to enforce safety online through the use of a runtime assurance mechanism.

6
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:55,680
A runtime assurance mechanism, which is sometimes also called an active set invariance filter, will filter a desired control signal online in order to balance performance goals with safety goals, such as invariance constraints.

7
00:00:55,790 --> 00:01:03,440
And in the figure on the bottom of the screen, we see an example where a nominal control policy will push the system outside of a safe set of operating conditions.

8
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:12,452
However, using a runtime assurance mechanism, we can ensure the forward invariance of the green region while also ensuring that our system trajectory resembles that of the nominal trajectory.

9
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:19,972
So numerous methods exist for enforcing safety at runtime, and in particular, control barrier function-based techniques are well suited for this task.

10
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:29,828
Certain control barrier function-based runtime assurance mechanisms even allow a system to leave its given safe set when safety can be verified by other means against alternate constraints.

11
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:40,836
However, these methods do not yet account for systems with disturbances, and in our work we aim to build in this functionality and create runtime assurance mechanisms for systems with disturbances.

12
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:50,468
So the main two contributions of our work are that we present a problem formulation for control barrier function-based runtime assurance for control systems with disturbances.

13
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:58,820
A controller which solves this problem statement must in some way compute reachable sets or assess the non-determinism in the system model by other means.

14
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:08,964
And to that end, we also present one solution to the problem statement where the non-determinism in the system model is mitigated using mixed monotonicity-based look ahead methods.

15
00:02:11,278 --> 00:02:18,160
I'll begin by addressing our first contribution, and we'll present a problem formulation for runtime assurance for control systems with disturbances.

16
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:29,120
In this setting, we're considering systems of the form given in equation one, where x is the state, u is the control input, and w is a disturbance input, which enters the system dynamics in an affine manner.

17
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:34,020
And additionally, we're going to pair the system with an unsafe set of states, which are to be avoided for all time.

18
00:02:35,278 --> 00:02:44,036
Additionally, we'll assume the existence of a backup control policy for our system, and this backup control policy is two parts a backup controller, UB, and a safe set, SB.

19
00:02:44,494 --> 00:02:51,652
The idea is that the backup controller renders the safe set forward invariant by means of a barrier which is known for the system.

20
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:56,004
And the safe set SB does not intersect the unsafe set.

21
00:02:56,240 --> 00:03:02,116
Thus, when beginning in the safe set and applying the backup controller for all time, safety is guaranteed for the system.

22
00:03:02,542 --> 00:03:11,268
However, we make no assumptions on the performance guarantees of the backup controller, and it may be such that the backup controller is not preferable to use an application.

23
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:14,948
And this is really the main motivation for the runtime assurance problem.

24
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:35,012
So this problem reads as follows: we're going to assume a system given by equation three, and we're going to pair it with an unsafe set of states, and we'll assume a safe, verified backup control policy for the system, and also a desired controller which satisfies some performance objective, but perhaps may cause the system to enter the obstacle region when employed.

25
00:03:35,406 --> 00:03:47,236
So the objective of the runtime assurance problem is to design a controller, UASIF, such that for any state beginning inside the safe set of the backup controller, the assured control input UASIF guarantees safety for all time.

26
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,644
But also UASIF evaluates the desired control input whenever it is safe to do so.

27
00:03:53,326 --> 00:03:56,720
Okay, so this is our first contribution and also our problem statement.

28
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:07,748
And in the remainder of this presentation, we'll discuss one potential solution to this problem statement where the non-determinism in the system model is assessed through the use of mixed monotonicity-based look ahead methods.

29
00:04:08,782 --> 00:04:14,692
Alright, so I'll just begin this segment by introducing some preliminaries on mixed monotone systems for those who are unfamiliar.

30
00:04:14,990 --> 00:04:22,436
So we'll just consider in this uh for the moment a non-deterministic system with no control input, so x dot is equal to f of x and w.

31
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:32,100
And this system is said to be mixed monotone when there exists a related decomposition function d, which separates the initial system dynamics into increasing and decreasing components.

32
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:36,160
Okay, and uh this this property has been shown to be quite general.

33
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:41,796
There exist special cases and algorithms for constructing decomposition functions given a particular system.

34
00:04:43,534 --> 00:04:53,444
This decomposition function then facilitates the construction of a deterministic auxiliary embedding system relative to the initial system, and this embedding system is monotone with respect to a particular order.

